As of the latest FDA materials identified, there are **two “most recent” FDA AI/ML medical-device guidances to distinguish**:

1. **Most recent AI-device FDA guidance overall — Draft, Jan. 6, 2025:**  
   FDA published **“Artificial Intelligence-Enabled Device Software Functions: Lifecycle Management and Marketing Submission Recommendations”** as a **draft guidance**. It proposes lifecycle-management considerations and marketing-submission recommendations for AI-enabled device software functions, including issues such as model development, validation, performance monitoring, and data drift. Source: FDA, *Artificial Intelligence in Software as a Medical Device* page, Jan. 6, 2025 notice: https://www.fda.gov/medical-devices/software-medical-device-samd/artificial-intelligence-software-medical-device

2. **Most recent final AI-device FDA guidance — PCCP guidance, Dec. 2024 / FDA guidance page:**  
   FDA finalized **“Marketing Submission Recommendations for a Predetermined Change Control Plan for Artificial Intelligence-Enabled Device Software Functions.”** This guidance recommends what to include in a **Predetermined Change Control Plan (PCCP)** for AI-enabled device software functions—i.e., the planned device modifications, the methodology to develop/validate/implement them, and an impact assessment—so that certain future AI modifications can be reviewed prospectively as part of the marketing submission. Source: FDA guidance page: https://www.fda.gov/regulatory-information/search-fda-guidance-documents/marketing-submission-recommendations-predetermined-change-control-plan-artificial-intelligence; Federal Register notice, Dec. 4, 2024: https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2024/12/04/2024-28361/marketing-submission-recommendations-for-a-predetermined-change-control-plan-for-artificial

## How this interacts with the EU AI Act

### 1. FDA clearance/approval and EU AI Act compliance are separate regimes

FDA guidance supports U.S. device review pathways—e.g., 510(k), De Novo, PMA—and focuses on reasonable assurance of safety and effectiveness for AI-enabled device software functions. The EU AI Act is an EU market-access and AI-governance regime. It applies to providers placing AI systems on the EU market or putting them into service in the EU, including non-EU providers where the system output is used in the EU. **EU AI Act, Article 2(1).**

So, an FDA-cleared AI medical device is **not automatically compliant** with the EU AI Act. It may reuse much of the same evidence, but it still needs EU conformity analysis, technical documentation, CE-marking alignment, and post-market obligations where applicable. **EU AI Act, Articles 16, 43, 47, 48.**

### 2. Many AI/ML medical devices will be “high-risk” in the EU

Under the EU AI Act, an AI system is high-risk where it is a safety component of, or is itself, a product covered by listed EU product-safety legislation and that product requires third-party conformity assessment. **EU AI Act, Article 6(1).** Medical devices and in vitro diagnostic medical devices are included through the EU product framework—principally the Medical Device Regulation and IVDR—listed in **EU AI Act, Annex I, Section A**.

In practical terms: if an AI diagnostic, triage, imaging, monitoring, or treatment-planning tool is regulated as medical-device software under EU MDR/IVDR and requires notified-body conformity assessment, it will generally be treated as a **high-risk AI system** under the EU AI Act. **EU AI Act, Article 6(1); Annex I, Section A.**

### 3. The EU AI Act layers AI-specific controls on top of MDR/IVDR

For high-risk AI systems, the provider must meet the AI Act’s high-risk requirements, including:

- risk management, **EU AI Act, Article 9**;
- data governance and representative training/validation/testing data, including bias assessment and mitigation, **Article 10**;
- technical documentation, **Article 11**;
- logging/record-keeping, **Article 12**;
- transparency and instructions for use, **Article 13**;
- human oversight, **Article 14**;
- accuracy, robustness, and cybersecurity throughout the lifecycle, **Article 15**;
- provider obligations such as QMS, conformity assessment, EU declaration of conformity, CE marking, registration, corrective actions, and authority cooperation, **Article 16**.

The Act expressly allows providers that already have a sectoral medical-device quality-management system to integrate AI Act QMS elements into that system. **EU AI Act, Article 17(3).** It also allows post-market monitoring elements to be integrated into existing sectoral post-market systems where the system is covered by Annex I product legislation, provided equivalent protection is achieved. **EU AI Act, Article 72(4).**

### 4. FDA PCCP maps conceptually to EU lifecycle and change-control duties, but is not a substitute

FDA’s PCCP guidance is especially relevant to AI/ML devices because it anticipates post-authorization model changes. In the EU, changes to a high-risk AI system can trigger continuing conformity duties and may create a new “provider” obligation if another actor makes a substantial modification. **EU AI Act, Article 25(1)(b).**

A PCCP can be useful evidence for EU compliance because it documents:

- planned AI modifications;
- validation methodology;
- performance evaluation;
- impact assessment;
- controls for post-market learning or updates.

Those artifacts align with EU AI Act requirements for lifecycle performance, robustness, cybersecurity, data governance, technical documentation, and post-market monitoring. **EU AI Act, Articles 10, 11, 15, 72.** But an FDA-accepted PCCP does **not** by itself determine whether an EU change is a “substantial modification” or whether MDR/IVDR notified-body review is needed. That must be assessed under the EU AI Act and applicable medical-device conformity rules. **EU AI Act, Articles 25, 43; Annex I, Section A.**

### Bottom line

The FDA’s newest AI-device work is the **Jan. 2025 draft lifecycle/marketing-submission guidance**, with the **PCCP guidance as the key final FDA guidance** for planned AI modifications. In the EU, the same AI medical device will often be a **high-risk AI system** if it is medical-device software or a safety component subject to MDR/IVDR third-party conformity assessment. The FDA framework and EU AI Act are compatible in many technical expectations—lifecycle control, validation, monitoring, change management, transparency, cybersecurity—but they are **not mutually substitutable**. FDA authorization supports U.S. marketing; EU AI Act/MDR-IVDR conformity supports EU market access.

